FILE: vUX-resizer.js
FEATURE: resizer-fixes
DATE: 2026-08-27
PRIOR STATE: as committed 2024-12-31, untouched since the v4.0.0-beta line began

The module was non-functional end to end. Three separate ReferenceErrors fired on
the first mousedown or the first config assignment, the drag arithmetic reduced to
an identity that ignored the drag entirely, and the component's own stylesheet was
never loaded so the handlers it creates had no size, no position and no cursor.
Every change below is a fix; no public API was removed.

================================================================================
CHANGE 1 — Declare callBack
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
    var pin=false, self=this, myResizeHandler=null, targetDirection=null, targetElementWidth=0,targetElementHeight=0,targetElement=$$.ss(elementSelector);

NEW_SECTION
    var pin=false, self=this, initialized=false, myResizeHandler=null, callBack=null, handlerClass="resizeHandle";
    var targetDirection=null, targetEdge=null, activeHandle=null, createdHandlers=[];
    var targetElementWidth=0, targetElementHeight=0, targetElement=null;
    //Drag origin: the pointer position and the element size at the moment the handle was grabbed.
    //Every mousemove is measured against these, so the box grows by how far the pointer travelled
    //rather than jumping to wherever the pointer happens to be.
    var startPointerX=0, startPointerY=0, startWidth=0, startHeight=0;

REASON
`callBack` was read in resize() and assigned in the config.callBack setter but never
declared anywhere. An ES module body is strict-mode code, so the setter's bare
assignment threw a ReferenceError the moment a consumer set config.callBack, and
resize()'s read threw one on every mousemove of every drag even when they did not.
The callback feature could therefore never fire, and its mere presence broke resizing.

The same declaration line picks up the state the rest of this feature needs: the
drag-origin quartet (change 3), the handler element actually grabbed (change 6), the
resolved handler class name (change 11), the list of handlers to remove on destroy
(change 9) and an initialized flag to make initialize() idempotent.

targetElement no longer resolves here — see change 9.

================================================================================
CHANGE 2 — Load the module's stylesheet
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
    (no equivalent — the module never linked a stylesheet)

NEW_SECTION
    async function addVitalStyles() {
        try {
            var path = await processAssetPath();

            if (!(path instanceof Error)){
                vModel.core.functions.linkStyleSheet(path+"css/resizer.css", "resizer");
            }else{
                throw new Error(path)
            }

        } catch (error) {
            console.error(error)
        }
    }

REASON
assets/css/resizer.css has existed since 2021 and carries everything that makes a
handler a handler: position:absolute, the 2px cross-axis thickness, the 100%
main-axis span, the four edge anchors (.x.left/.x.right/.y.top/.y.bottom), the
col-resize/row-resize cursors and the default :hover/.on highlight. Nothing linked
it. A consumer following the documented usage got zero-size static DIVs appended to
their container — an invisible, ungrabbable component — unless they happened to
supply a complete style string through config.resizeHandlerProperties.styles.

Loading follows the established pattern rather than inventing one: the same
processAssetPath()/linkStyleSheet() shape used by vUX-modalDisplayer.js and
vUX-formComponents.js, so the file resolves through vModel.core.data.assetPath and
honours the data-library-root contract instead of a hardcoded relative path.

================================================================================
CHANGE 3 — Resize against the drag origin instead of the raw pointer coordinate
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
    function resize(e){
        if(pin){
            if(targetDirection == "x"){
                var newWidth = targetElementWidth + (e.clientX - targetElementWidth);
                var maxWidth = thresholdValues.maxWidth;
                var minWidth = thresholdValues.minWidth;
                if(maxWidth != 0){
                    newWidth = newWidth > maxWidth? maxWidth:newWidth;
                }
                if(minWidth != 0){
                    newWidth = newWidth < minWidth? minWidth:newWidth;
                }
                
                targetElement.style["width"] = newWidth+"px";
                targetElementWidth = newWidth;
            }else{
                var newHeight = targetElementHeight + (e.clientY - targetElementHeight);

NEW_SECTION
    function resize(e){
        if(pin){
            if(targetDirection == "x"){
                //A handle sitting on the left edge grows the element when the pointer moves left,
                //so the travelled distance is inverted for that edge.
                var delta = (targetEdge == "left")? (startPointerX - e.clientX) : (e.clientX - startPointerX);
                var newWidth = startWidth + delta;
                var maxWidth = thresholdValues.maxWidth;
                var minWidth = thresholdValues.minWidth;
                if(maxWidth != 0){
                    newWidth = newWidth > maxWidth? maxWidth:newWidth;
                }
                if(minWidth != 0){
                    newWidth = newWidth < minWidth? minWidth:newWidth;
                }
                if(newWidth < 0) newWidth = 0;

                targetElement.style["width"] = newWidth+"px";
                targetElementWidth = newWidth;
            }else{
                var delta = (targetEdge == "top")? (startPointerY - e.clientY) : (e.clientY - startPointerY);
                var newHeight = startHeight + delta;

REASON
`targetElementWidth + (e.clientX - targetElementWidth)` cancels algebraically to
`e.clientX`. The element's new width was therefore the pointer's distance from the
left edge of the *viewport*, which is not a width of anything. Three consequences,
all reported as the component "jumping": the element snapped to the cursor on the
first mousemove instead of following it; the grab offset within the handler was
discarded, so grabbing a handler anywhere but its exact left edge shifted the box;
and any element not flush against the viewport's left edge was resized by its own
page offset plus its width. The Y branch had the identical defect against clientY,
which additionally meant an element part-way down a page could never be made short.

The replacement is the standard drag model: record the pointer position and the
element's size at mousedown (change 5), then set size = startSize + how far the
pointer has travelled since. The grab offset cancels out of the subtraction, so
where inside the handler the drag begins no longer matters, and the element's
position on the page no longer enters the arithmetic at all.

The edge inversion is new behaviour that the old code had no way to express. A
handler anchored to the left or top edge moves toward the element's origin as the
pointer moves in the negative direction, which should make the element bigger, not
smaller. Without the inversion, position:{x:"left"} produced a control that shrank
the element when dragged outward — backwards, and the reason the left/right and
top/bottom position options were only ever usable in one of their two settings.

`if(newWidth < 0) newWidth = 0` guards the case the threshold clamps do not cover.
minWidth of 0 is the documented "unset" sentinel, so a configuration with no minimum
had nothing stopping a fast drag past the far edge from writing a negative width,
which the browser rejects, leaving the element stuck at its last valid size mid-drag.

================================================================================
CHANGE 4 — Bind removable listeners that tolerate a child as the event target
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
        $$.attachEventHandler("mousedown", "resizeHandle", pinPoint);
        addEventListener("mouseup", unPin);
        addEventListener("mousemove", resize);

NEW_SECTION
        //Bound as named listeners rather than through $$.attachEventHandler() so that destroy()
        //can detach them again, and so that a mousedown landing on a child of the handle still counts.
        document.addEventListener("mousedown", pinPoint);
        document.addEventListener("mouseup", unPin);
        document.addEventListener("mousemove", resize);

REASON
$$.attachEventHandler() registers an anonymous wrapper function against window, so
there is no reference with which to unregister it — a component using it can never
be torn down, which is what blocked destroy() (change 9) from existing. It also
matches on e.target's own class list only, so a mousedown landing on any child of a
handler was ignored; that is invisible for the empty DIVs this module builds itself,
but it silently broke config.myResizeHandler for any consumer whose handler contains
a grip glyph or an icon.

Binding pinPoint by name keeps both problems out. The class match it used to perform
moves into resolveHandler() (change 5), which walks up from e.target via closest()
and so accepts a descendant. Listening on document rather than window is what makes
that walk possible, since window is not a node.

The two window-level listeners move to document alongside it purely for symmetry of
removal; mouseup and mousemove bubble to both.

================================================================================
CHANGE 5 — Capture the drag origin; resolve axis and edge from the handler
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
    function pinPoint(e){
        if(!pin){
            targetElementleWidth = parseInt($$.sm(targetElement).cssStyle("width"));
            targetElementleHeight = parseInt($$.sm(targetElement).cssStyle("height"));
            e.target.classList.toggle("on");
            if(e.target.classList.contains("x")){
                targetDirection = "x";
            }else{
                targetDirection = "y";
            }
            targetDirection
            pin=true;
        }
    }

NEW_SECTION
    function resolveHandler(node){
        if(node == null || node.classList == null) return null;
        if(node.classList.contains(handlerClass)) return node;
        return (typeof node.closest == "function")? node.closest("."+handlerClass) : null;
    }
    function resolveDirection(handler){
        if(handler.classList.contains("x")) return "x";
        if(handler.classList.contains("y")) return "y";
        //A consumer-supplied handler need not carry the axis marker class as long as the
        //configured direction leaves only one possibility.
        if(resizeHandlerProperties.direction != "both") return resizeHandlerProperties.direction;
        throw new Error("Resizer cannot tell which axis the handler resizes, give the handler element an 'x' or a 'y' class, or set 'resizerObj.config.resizeHandlerProperties.direction' to 'x' or 'y'");
    }
    function resolveEdge(handler, direction){
        var edges = (direction == "x")? ["left", "right"] : ["top", "bottom"];
        if(handler.classList.contains(edges[0])) return edges[0];
        if(handler.classList.contains(edges[1])) return edges[1];
        return resizeHandlerProperties.position[direction];
    }
    function pinPoint(e){
        if(!pin){
            var handler = resolveHandler(e.target);
            if(handler == null) return;
            e.preventDefault(); //keeps the drag from turning into a text selection
            targetDirection = resolveDirection(handler);
            targetEdge = resolveEdge(handler, targetDirection);
            startWidth = parseInt($$.sm(targetElement).cssStyle("width"));
            startHeight = parseInt($$.sm(targetElement).cssStyle("height"));
            //A computed size of "auto" parses to NaN, fall back to the rendered box.
            if(isNaN(startWidth)) startWidth = targetElement.offsetWidth;
            if(isNaN(startHeight)) startHeight = targetElement.offsetHeight;
            targetElementWidth = startWidth;
            targetElementHeight = startHeight;
            startPointerX = e.clientX;
            startPointerY = e.clientY;
            activeHandle = handler;
            handler.classList.add("on");
            pin=true;
        }
    }

REASON
`targetElementleWidth` and `targetElementleHeight` are misspellings ("le" for the
final "l") of the module's own variables. Being undeclared in strict-mode module
code, both assignments threw a ReferenceError, so pinPoint() aborted on its very
first statement. Nothing after it ran: no direction was chosen, `pin` was never set,
and the component did not respond to a mousedown at all. This is the defect that
made the module inert rather than merely wrong. The measured sizes now land in the
drag-origin variables change 3 reads, and are mirrored into targetElementWidth/Height
so the resizedWidth/resizedHeight getters report the true size from mousedown onward
instead of 0 until the first mousemove.

Measuring with parseInt on a computed style yields NaN when the element has no
explicit size and the computed value comes back "auto"; startWidth of NaN propagates
through the arithmetic and writes "NaNpx", which the browser discards. The offsetWidth
fallback covers the common case of a container that has never been sized.

Direction detection used to read `e.target.classList` and treat "not x" as y. Because
$$.attachEventHandler had already narrowed e.target to something carrying the handler
class this was survivable for built-in handlers, but a consumer handler with neither
marker class silently resized on the Y axis rather than reporting the ambiguity.
resolveDirection() now falls back to the configured direction when it is unambiguous,
which is what actually makes config.myResizeHandler usable (change 10), and throws a
descriptive error naming both remedies when direction is "both" and the handler is
unmarked — a case with no correct guess available.

resolveEdge() is new, supplying change 3's inversion. It prefers the edge class on
the handler so a hand-written handler is self-describing, and falls back to the
configured position for handlers that carry no edge class.

preventDefault() stops the browser beginning a text selection at mousedown. Without
it a drag across the page painted every element it crossed in selection highlight,
and released over text, left that text selected.

The bare `targetDirection` expression statement is deleted; it evaluated a variable
and discarded the result.

classList.toggle("on") is replaced by add("on") — see change 6.

================================================================================
CHANGE 6 — Release the handler that was actually grabbed
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
    function unPin(){
        if(pin){
            pin=false;
            $$.ss(".resizeHandle").classList.toggle("on");
        }
    }

NEW_SECTION
    function unPin(){
        if(pin){
            pin=false;
            //Clear the handler that was actually grabbed, not simply the first one in the document.
            if(activeHandle != null) activeHandle.classList.remove("on");
            activeHandle=null;
        }
    }

REASON
$$.ss() is document.querySelector, so this cleared the "on" class from the first
.resizeHandle in document order regardless of which one the user had dragged. With
direction:"both", or with two Resizer instances on one page, releasing the Y handler
un-highlighted the X handler instead: the released handler stayed lit permanently
while an untouched one lost a class it never had.

The toggle/toggle pairing made that worse than a cosmetic slip. Because both ends
toggled rather than set, the class tracked the parity of the drag count rather than
the drag state, so highlighting drifted further out of step with reality on every
drag. add() on grab and remove() on release makes the state absolute, and remembering
the grabbed element in activeHandle makes it the right element.

================================================================================
CHANGE 7 — Build handlers with axis-scoped styles, honouring styles.both
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
        if(direction == "both"){
            xhandler = $$.ce("DIV", {class: "resizeHandle x "+xPosition});
            yhandler = $$.ce("DIV", {class: "resizeHandle y "+yPosition});
        }else{
            if(direction == "x"){
                xhandler = $$.ce("DIV", {class: "resizeHandle x "+xPosition});
            }else{
                yhandler = $$.ce("DIV", {class: "resizeHandle y "+yPosition});
            }
        }

        if(xhandler != null){
            var css = ".resizeHandle:hover{"+xStyle+"}";
            css += ".resizeHandle.on{"+xStyle+"}";
            if(xStyle != "") attachStyleSheet("xResizehandler", css);
            parent.appendChild(xhandler);
        }

        if(yhandler != null){
            var css = ".resizeHandle:hover{"+yStyle+"}";
            css += ".resizeHandle.on{"+yStyle+"}";
            if(yhandler != "") attachStyleSheet("yResizehandler", css);
            parent.appendChild(yhandler);
        }

NEW_SECTION
    function buildHandler(parent, axis, position, style){
        var handler = $$.ce("DIV", {class: handlerClass+" "+axis+" "+position});
        if(style != ""){
            //Scoped to the axis, otherwise the x and y rules overwrite one another.
            var selector = "."+handlerClass+"."+axis;
            var css = selector+":hover{"+style+"}";
            css += selector+".on{"+style+"}";
            attachStyleSheet(axis+"Resizehandler", css);
        }
        parent.appendChild(handler);
        createdHandlers.push(handler);
    }
    function createResizeHandler(parent){
        if (!$$.sm(parent).isPositioned()) parent.style["position"] = "relative";
        var direction = resizeHandlerProperties.direction;
        var styles    = resizeHandlerProperties.styles;

        if(direction == "x" || direction == "both"){
            buildHandler(parent, "x", resizeHandlerProperties.position.x, (styles.x != "")? styles.x : styles.both);
        }
        if(direction == "y" || direction == "both"){
            buildHandler(parent, "y", resizeHandlerProperties.position.y, (styles.y != "")? styles.y : styles.both);
        }
    }

REASON
Four defects in one block.

`if(yhandler != "")` tests the Y handler *element* against an empty string where it
meant to test yStyle, as the X branch correctly does one block above. An element is
never equal to "", so the guard was permanently true and an empty yStyle emitted
`.resizeHandle:hover{}` — harmless in itself, but it is the rule that then collided
with the X rule below.

Both branches generated their rules against the bare `.resizeHandle` selector rather
than `.resizeHandle.x` / `.resizeHandle.y`. With direction:"both" the two stylesheets
had identical specificity and both applied to both handlers, so whichever was
appended second won and the styles.x/styles.y distinction did nothing. Combined with
the always-true guard above, configuring only styles.x and leaving styles.y empty
produced an empty Y rule that landed last and blanked the X styling. Scoping each
rule to its axis makes the two independent.

resizeHandlerProperties.styles.both was accepted and validated by the config setter
but read by nothing — a documented key with no effect. It now supplies the style for
either axis that has no style of its own, which is the only reading under which the
key means anything.

The branching is flattened to two independent conditions. The old shape had "both"
duplicate the two element-creation lines verbatim, and the else-branch treat any
non-"x" direction as "y"; expressing each axis as its own test removes the
duplication and the implicit fallthrough.

Handlers are recorded in createdHandlers so destroy() can remove them (change 9),
and are built with handlerClass rather than the literal so a custom class name
(change 11) is honoured.

================================================================================
CHANGE 8 — (folded into change 7)
================================================================================

The xhandler/yhandler/xStyle/yStyle locals and the xPosition/yPosition locals
disappear with the rewrite above; each is now a buildHandler() argument.

================================================================================
CHANGE 9 — Resolve the target in initialize(); add destroy()
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
    this.initialize = function(){
        addEventHanler();
    }

NEW_SECTION
    this.initialize = function(){
        if(initialized) return;
        //Resolved here rather than in the constructor so a target added to the DOM after
        //construction still works, and so a missing target reports itself instead of failing later.
        targetElement = $$.ss(elementSelector);
        validateElement(targetElement, "Resizer() found no element matching the selector '"+elementSelector+"'");
        addVitalStyles();
        addEventHanler();
        initialized = true;
    }
    this.destroy = function(){
        if(!initialized) return;
        unPin();
        document.removeEventListener("mousedown", pinPoint);
        document.removeEventListener("mouseup", unPin);
        document.removeEventListener("mousemove", resize);
        for(var i=0; i<createdHandlers.length; i++){
            if(createdHandlers[i].parentNode != null) createdHandlers[i].parentNode.removeChild(createdHandlers[i]);
        }
        createdHandlers = [];
        initialized = false;
    }

REASON
targetElement was resolved in the constructor, and never checked. A selector matching
nothing stored null and reported nothing; the failure surfaced much later as a
TypeError inside $$.sm() on the first mousedown, pointing at the library rather than
at the consumer's selector. Resolving in initialize() also means a target created
after the Resizer is constructed now works, which matches how the other modules in
this library defer their DOM lookups, and validateElement() names the offending
selector in the error the way the module's other validators name their arguments.

The initialized guard makes initialize() idempotent. Calling it twice previously
appended a second set of handlers, registered a second set of listeners and appended
a duplicate <style> element for each axis — attachStyleSheet() appends unconditionally.

destroy() is new. Every listener this module registers was previously permanent, and
the handlers it injects into the consumer's container had no removal path, so a
Resizer over an element belonging to a view — an SPA route, a modal — leaked its
listeners and left orphaned handler DIVs behind on every teardown. It could not be
written while binding went through $$.attachEventHandler (change 4). unPin() runs
first so a destroy mid-drag does not leave a handler stuck in its "on" state.

================================================================================
CHANGE 10 — Fix the resizeHandlerProperties key count and unknown-key handling
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
                var totalTargetKeys = targetConfig[0].length;
...
                targetConfig.forEach(function (config){
                    config[0] != config[0].toLowerCase();
                    if(config[0] == "styles"){
                        validateObjectLiteral(config[1], "resizerObj.config.resizeHandlerProperties.position property must be an object literal");
                        var target = Object.entries(config[1]);
                        var totalPositionKeys = target.length;
                        if(totalPositionKeys > 3 ) throw new Error ("resizerObj.config.resizeHandlerProperties.position keys must not be more than 3");

                        target.forEach(function (element){
                            element[0] = element[0].toLowerCase();
                            if (element[0] != "x" && element[0] != "y" && element[0] != "both") throw new Error ("resizerObj.config.resizeHandlerProperties.position keys can either be 'x', 'y' or 'both'");
                            validateString(element[1], "resizerObj.config.resizeHandlerProperties.position."+element[0]+" property value must be a string");
...
                    }else if(config[0] == "direction"){
                        ...
                        resizeHandlerProperties[config[0]] = config[1];
                    }
                });

NEW_SECTION
                var totalTargetKeys = targetConfig.length;
...
                targetConfig.forEach(function (config){
                    config[0] = config[0].toLowerCase();
                    if(config[0] == "styles"){
                        validateObjectLiteral(config[1], "resizerObj.config.resizeHandlerProperties.styles property must be an object literal");
                        var target = Object.entries(config[1]);
                        var totalStyleKeys = target.length;
                        if(totalStyleKeys > 3 ) throw new Error ("resizerObj.config.resizeHandlerProperties.styles keys must not be more than 3");

                        target.forEach(function (element){
                            element[0] = element[0].toLowerCase();
                            if (element[0] != "x" && element[0] != "y" && element[0] != "both") throw new Error ("resizerObj.config.resizeHandlerProperties.styles keys can either be 'x', 'y' or 'both'");
                            validateString(element[1], "resizerObj.config.resizeHandlerProperties.styles."+element[0]+" property value must be a string");
...
                    }else if(config[0] == "direction"){
                        ...
                        resizeHandlerProperties[config[0]] = config[1];
                    }else{
                        throw new Error("resizerObj.config.resizeHandlerProperties keys must be one of the followings: "+ validKeys.join(", ") + " '"+config[0] + "' is not one of them");
                    }
                });

REASON
`targetConfig[0].length` is the length of the first [key, value] pair produced by
Object.entries, which is always 2 — not the number of keys supplied. The guard
below it therefore compared 2 against 4 and 0 on every call: it never rejected an
over-long object, and it could never see the empty object it was written to catch.
Passing {} instead made `targetConfig[0]` undefined and threw a bare TypeError on
the property read, one line before the descriptive error meant to handle exactly
that case.

`config[0] != config[0].toLowerCase();` is a comparison whose result is discarded,
where the surrounding code (and the inner loops) use assignment to normalise a key.
Key matching was thus case-sensitive against lowercase literals, so {Direction:"y"}
matched no branch.

Matching no branch was silent: the if/else-if chain had no final else. A misspelled
or miscased key was accepted, ignored, and the component ran on with its defaults —
the single hardest failure mode to diagnose from the consumer's side, and contrary
to the strict-validation contract every other setter in this file follows. The added
else throws in the same form as the thresholdValues setter's unknown-key error.

The "styles" branch was a copy of the "position" branch with its error strings left
unchanged, so every diagnostic it raised named `position` while validating `styles`,
including the key list, which is the wrong list ('x', 'y', 'both' are not position
keys). Its local was named totalPositionKeys for the same reason. Strings and local
corrected to say styles; no logic change in this part.

================================================================================
CHANGE 11 — Make config.myResizeHandler actually take effect
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
        myResizeHandler:{
            set: function(value) {
                validateString(value, "resizerObj.config.myResizeHandler property value must be a string");
                myResizeHandler = value;
            }
        },

NEW_SECTION
        myResizeHandler:{
            set: function(value) {
                validateString(value, "resizerObj.config.myResizeHandler property value must be a string");
                myResizeHandler = value;
                //The consumer names their own handler class, drop a leading dot so both
                //"handle" and ".handle" are accepted.
                handlerClass = value.replace(/^\./, "");
            }
        },

REASON
myResizeHandler was validated and stored, and read in exactly one place — the
`myResizeHandler == null` test that decides whether to build handlers. Its value was
never used for anything. Binding was hardcoded to the literal class "resizeHandle",
so a consumer who supplied their own handler got the intended half of the behaviour
(no handlers injected) and not the other half (nothing listening to theirs): the
config option's entire purpose was to produce a component that did nothing.

Recording the value into handlerClass, which resolveHandler() matches against and
buildHandler() writes, closes the loop. Accepting a leading dot is a small tolerance
for the natural reading of "class name" as a CSS selector; the value is concatenated
into a selector in resolveHandler() and into a class attribute in buildHandler(), and
those two want it written differently.

With change 5's direction fallback, a consumer handler now needs no vUX marker
classes at all when direction is "x" or "y".

================================================================================
VERIFICATION
================================================================================

There is no test runner in this repository and no Resizer example page to drive, so
verification was static plus an extracted-arithmetic check.

  - Module parses as an ES module (node --input-type=module, core import stubbed).
  - The delta arithmetic from change 3 was lifted verbatim into a standalone script
    and exercised over 8 cases: right-edge grow and shrink, left-edge grow and
    shrink (the inversion), clamping at maxWidth, clamping at minWidth, the negative
    guard, and a zero-distance drag leaving the size untouched. All 8 pass. The same
    cases against the old expression return the pointer coordinate in every one.
  - Grepped the fixed file for each original defect signature to confirm none
    survives: targetElementleWidth, targetElementleHeight,
    "e.clientX - targetElementWidth", 'yhandler != ""', "targetConfig[0].length",
    '$$.ss(".resizeHandle")' and 'classList.toggle("on")' are all absent.

Not verified: real mouse-drag behaviour in a browser, and that resizer.css resolves
at runtime under a live data-library-root. Both need a page to load the module, and
the Resizer module has no example under examples/ — writing one is the obvious next
piece of work and would turn every check above into something demonstrable.
